;; peer-pid-accessor-naming.wat — the naming target for the pid accessor (arc 170 capability circuit, stone 2).
;;
;; THE THING BEING NAMED: a :wat::kernel:: accessor that, given a Peer value, lifts the OS pid of the
;; process on the FAR END of that peer — or None when the far end is not a separate process.
;;
;;   process peer (the far end is a forked child process)  -> (Some child-pid)   ; the kernel-vouched pid
;;   thread  peer (the far end is a cell in THIS process)  -> :None              ; no separate process
;;
;; The Option return is load-bearing: it encodes the FIRM MEMORY BOUNDARY itself —
;;   Some = a process on the far side  = grantable (its pid keys the SO_PEERCRED allow-set)
;;   None = in-process (a thread cell)  = no grant needed (the handle IS the capability)
;;
;; WHAT THE PID IS (so the name is honest about it):
;;   - the pid of the peer's far-end PROCESS, read from the kernel Pidfd the peer holds
;;     (unforgeable — a Pidfd is only mintable at fork; it cannot be built from a raw pid).
;;   - used purely as an IDENTITY for the capability allow-set (grant/revoke via SO_PEERCRED),
;;     NEVER for kill()/signalling.
;;   - the SAME kernel handle the reap uses (PID-reuse-safe) — the pinned identity of that child.
;;
;; SIBLING VERBS it sits beside (the :wat::kernel:: peer surface — anchors for register/tone):
;;   (:wat::kernel::send    peer msg)      ; -> nil
;;   (:wat::kernel::recv    peer)          ; -> msg
;;   (:wat::kernel::connect addr)          ; -> Peer
;;   (:wat::kernel::self-peer out in)       ; -> ThreadSelfPeer
;;   (:wat::kernel::allow   listener pid)  ; the process-tier accept-gate mutation
;;   (:wat::kernel::deny    listener pid)
;;
;; NB the existing record already spells the pid of a freshly-forked child, owner-side:
;;   (:wat::core::defrecord :wat::spawn::ProcessLaunch [pid <- :wat::core::i64])   ; field named `pid`

;; ── CANDIDATE NAMES (which one KEEPS ITS PROMISE — says what it is, at the use site?) ──────────
;;
;; The use site reads: (:wat::kernel::<name> p)  where p is a (Peer :- [S R]), yielding (Option i64).
;;
;;   pid          — bare. Beside send/recv it reads as "the pid of this peer" — but WHOSE pid?
;;                  (the far-end process's, not mine). Does the bare word carry "far end + Option"?
;;   peer-pid     — "the peer's pid". Names the subject (the peer) but is the pid the PEER's or the
;;                  peer's FAR END's? A peer is a channel; the pid is the process it talks to.
;;   remote-pid   — "the pid of the remote end". `remote` says far-side; but a thread peer's far end
;;                  is in-process (not remote) — does `remote` mislead for the None case, or does the
;;                  None answer ("no remote process") make `remote` exactly right?
;;   answerer-pid — the SO_PEERCRED / accept-gate language (address.rs speaks of the "answerer pid");
;;                  precise to the security model, but is "answerer" obvious to a first reader?
;;   far-pid      — terse "far end pid"; obvious? or too cute?
;;
;; (Prime `'` convention: the peer verbs carry a trailing `'` — send/recv/connect. Should the
;;  accessor? It reads, not sends/rendezvous — weigh whether the `'` belongs.)

(:wat::core::defn :naming::PROBE
  [p <- :wat::kernel::Peer<:wat::core::i64,:wat::core::i64>]
  -> (:wat::core::Option :wat::core::i64)
  ;; <name> goes here — pick the one whose promise holds at THIS use site:
  (:wat::kernel::PLACEHOLDER-NAME p))
